T1  - Mindful sound therapy, alexithymia & fractals.
T3  - Dissertation Abstracts International
A1  - Noel, Alix Guery

Y1  - 2026//
N2  - Sound therapies have shown positive outcomes on tension, anxiety, and depressed mood (Goldsby et al., 2017), as well as on stress, heart rate, and blood pressure (Landry, 2014). These therapies often incorporate meditative states and traditional instruments like Himalayan singing bowls (Goldsby et al., 2017). Moreover, studies indicate that meditative sounds influence brain activity through fractal dimension analysis (Harne, 2014; Sanyal et al., 2024).This dissertation examines whether those therapies-defined as mindful therapies when administered by professional energy therapists (Reiki, polarity therapy)-can aid in treating alexithymia, a condition characterized by impaired emotional recognition. The study measured qualitative effects alongside quantitative assessments using electrophotonic imaging (EPI) and electroencephalogram (EEG) fractal dimension analysis. Alexithymia affects 8- 15% of the population (de Haan et al., 2013, p. 911) and is linked to depression (Bamonti et al., 2010), alcoholism, schizophrenia (de Haan et al., 2013), and post-traumatic stress disorder (Frewen et al., 2008). It also correlates with reduced limbic-frontal connectivity, which contributes to violent behavior and psychopathy (Gonzalez, 2017) and is considered difficult to treat (Kristiansson, 1995). Addressing alexithymia could help mitigate these conditions.In this study, 30 therapy sessions were conducted with 18 participants (ages 25-65). After treatment, the Difficulty Identifying Feelings (DIF) component of the TAS score significantly decreased (p = .04), with a moderate reduction in overall TAS scores (p = .08), particularly among highly alexithymic participants (p = .04). EEG fractal dimension increased significantly (p = .026), suggesting enhanced neural criticality. Spectral analysis showed significant increases in the alpha (p = .046), beta (p = .034), and gamma (p = .035) bands, and greater synchrony (p < 0.01), suggesting improved emotional processing. EPI stress levels significantly decreased (p = .0006), while alignment and energy levels increased (p = .01, p = .0007). The correlation between EPI and EEG fractal dimension strengthened (r = -.16 to r = -.49), suggesting improved emotional adaptability, neural complexity and embodiment. Qualitative data revealed enhanced emotional regulation, social interactions, bodily awareness, and well-being. Notably, participants reported increased self-awareness and improved relationships, supporting the hypothesis that self-awareness promotes emotional and social well-being. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
