ah, Reilly, T., Atkinson, G., Budgett, R., Reilly, Reilly, T., Waterhouse, J., Edwards, B., Reilly, Rubinov, M., Sporns, O., Rubinov, Sack, R. L., Sack, Shao, Y., Wang, L., Ye, E., Jin, X., Ni, W., Yang, Y., Yang, Z., ..., Shao, Spitzer, R. L., Terman, M., Williams, J. B. W., Terman, J. S., Malt, U. F., Singer, F., Lewy, A. J., Spitzer, Sporns, O., Tononi, G., Kotter, R., Sporns, Sterpenich, V., Albouy, G., Boly, M., Vandewalle, G., Darsaud, A., Balteau, E., Maquet, P., ..., Sterpenich, Thomas, M., Sing, H., Belenky, G., Holcomb, H., Mayberg, H., Dannals, R., Redmond, D., ..., Thomas, Wahlin, A., Bunce, D., Wahlin, T. B., Wahlin, Waterhouse, J., Reilly, T., Atkinson, G., Waterhouse, Watts, D. J., Strogatz, S. H., Watts, Wright, J. E., Vogel, J. A., Sampson, J. B., Knapik, J. J., Patton, J. F., Daniels, W. L., Wright, Zhang, J., Wang, J., Wu, Q., Kuang, W., Huang, X., He, Y., Gong, Q., Zhang, Zhao, Y., Li, J., Liu, X., Song, Y., Wang, R., Yang, Z., Liu, J., Zhao, Zhu, Y., Feng, Z., Xu, J., Fu, C., Sun, J., Yang, X., Qin, W., ..., Zhu, Zisapel, N., Zisapel
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Jet lag is commonly experienced when travelers cross multiple time zones, leaving the wake-sleep cycle and intrinsic biological "clocks" out of synchrony with the current environment. The effect of jet lag on intrinsic cortical function remains unclear. Twenty-two healthy individuals experiencing west-to-east jet lag flight were recruited. Brain structural and functional magnetic resonance studies, as well as psychological and neurohormonal tests, were carried out when participants returned from travel over six time zones and 50 days later when their jet lag symptoms had resolved. During jet lag, the functional brain network exhibited a small-world topology that was shifted toward regularity. Alterations during jet lag relative to recovery included decreased basal ganglia-thalamocortical network connections and increased functional connectivity between the medial temporal lobe subsystem and medial visual cortex. The lower melatonin and higher thyroid hormone levels during jet lag showed the same trend as brain activity in the right lingual gyrus. Although there was no significant difference between cortisol measurements during and after jet lag, cortisol levels were associated with temporal lobe activity in the jet lag condition. Brain and neuroendocrine changes during jet lag were related to jet lag symptoms. Further prospective studies are needed to explore the time course over which jet lag acts on the human brain. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
