., Milanovic, S., Tsuang, M.T., Faraone, S.V., McCarley, R.W., Seidman, L.J., Whitfield-Gabrieli, Winnicott, D.W., Winnicott, Yang, X.F., Bossmann, J., Schiffhauer, B., Jordan, M., Immordino-Yang, M.H., Yang
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - The present study aims at reviewing how and if resting state brain activity and a psychoanalytical perspective could elucidate the putative relationship between creativity and psychiatric disorders. Psychiatric disorders share sometimes higher levels of creativity with genius-like subjects. So creativity is not always enhanced and useful, but it is sometimes compromised toward specific psychiatric disorders, as we said above. Among various forms of creativity and in particular of divergent thinking, free association brings to the discovery of the unconscious other. This last could be said as this analytic perspective achievement of thinking other than consciously recognised. More studies are needed to understand if perhaps mental disorders could be thought as an alter-ego of this frame, where creativity has not been canalized. More systematic investigations are needed to understand the link between creativity and mental illness such as psychosis and affective disorders. Based on the present literature review, we conclude that, although expansive literature is available on the topic, the link between mental illness and creativity remains far from clear. This may be partly due to the fact that this link has not been investigate systematically and without a clear consensus about underlying models. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
