Y1  - 2016//
N2  - Comments on an article by A.Thul et al. (see record 2015-46195-001). Thul et al. investigated cortical information processing in 15 patients with chronic disorders of consciousness following a range of aetiologies. Specifically, they quantified the local information content of the electroencephalogram by means of permutation entropy, and the flow of information between cerebral lobes by means of symbolic transfer entropy. Thul et al. observe differences between patients and healthy control participants, they do not observe reliable differences between the levels of the disorder i.e. between vegetative state and minimally conscious state. Thul et al. report that the foci of the complexity decrements were consistent with each patient's specific pathology - i.e. focally reduced information content over left-frontal scalp in one patient with left-frontal subdural hematoma. There is evidence that oddball tasks, such as the own-name paradigm employed by Thul et al. have both diagnostic and prognostic value. Therefore, the combination of classical ERP analyses and complexity metrics of the same data may provide a greater level of clinical utility. Thul et al. employed the gold standard behavioural assessment tool for these disorders, namely the Coma Recovery Scale - Revised, which is known to result in greater diagnostic accuracy. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
