T1  - Scalp-EEG fast activity synchronization precedes nosewiping in sleep-arousal in temporal lobe epilepsy.
A1  - Zhu, Peimin

A2  - Geyer, J.D., Payne, T.A., Faught, E., Drury, I., Geyer, Gnatkovsky, V., de Curtis, M., Pastori, C., Cardinale, F., Lo Russo, G., Mai, R., et al., Gnatkovsky, Gotman, J., Levtova, V., Olivier, A., Gotman, Hirsch, L.J., Lain, A.H., Walczak, T.S., Hirsch, Wennberg, R., Wennberg
Y1  - 2015//
N2  - Nosewiping has been studied as a reliable localization sign in temporal lobe epilepsy (TLE), especially with mesial temporal lobe onset. Postictal nosewiping performed exclusively with one hand occurs in approximately 50% of patients with TLE and is highly predictive of seizure onset ipsilateral to the hand used, especially when it occurs repetitively. Reported in this paper are two patients undergoing video-electroencephalography monitoring who had nosewiping in sleep preceded with continuous epileptiform discharges in the temporal lobe region. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
