Y1  - 2012//
N2  - This editorial is a collection of papers which discusses about sleep spindles, mobile phones, lucid dreaming and sleep in Parkinson's disease and autism spectrum disorders. Memory consolidation is one well-researched aspect of brain function and in the current issue of Journal of Sleep Research (JSR )Holz and colleagues describe simple correlations between EEG characteristics in NREM sleep and consolidation of declarative memory as assessed by a word list and procedural memory as assessed by a mirror tracing task. The authors speculate that these differences between tonic and phasic REM sleep may be important for differences in memory consolidation or information processing during these two stages of REM sleep. The authors link this age dependency to brain maturation which according to the literature is also reflected in the sleep EEG, and not just in SWA but also in phase synchrony and gamma band power. In this issue some evidence was obtained that addition of cognitive behavioral therapy could enhance the effects of melatonin. This issue consist papers which describes how sleep deprivation affects alerting, orientating and executive attention, but not differentially. It is reported how sleep misperception in insomnia correlates with the multiple sleep latency test. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
