Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Comments on an article by J. J. Gonzalez et al. (see record 2011-04780-030). The authors have adopted one measure of inter-areal interaction, phase synchrony, to assess the maturation of large-scale communication within brain networks in term babies. The data of Gonzalez et al. suggest that oscillations in remote brain areas may be phase-correlated with high temporal accuracy and that this synchrony is, to some degree, specific to developmental stages, frequency bands, and brain areas. In conclusion, the study paves the way in neonatal EEG field by exploiting a powerful new analysis paradigm. At the same time, clinical and basic neurobiology are pointing towards the idea that a well functioning brain must have proper functional connectivity. EEG synchrony is not a trivial task to measure, but the underlying physics of neonatal EEG makes it more suited to such analysis than EEG from any older age. Despite of remaining mathematical challenges, analysis tools for EEG synchrony are now available for researchers to develop genuine bedside studies of functional networks in a newborn brain. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
