T1  - A cautionary note on the interpretation of phase-locking estimates with concurrent changes in power.
T3  - Electroencephalography & Clinical Neurophysiology
A1  - Muthukumaraswamy, Suresh D
A1  - Singh, Krish D

A2  - Aviyente, S., Bernat, E.M., Evans, W.S., Sponheim, S.R., Aviyente, Lachaux, J.P., Rodriguez, E., Martinerie, J., Varela, F.J., Lachaux, Le Van Quyen, M., Foucher, J., Lachaux, J.P., Rodriguez, E., Lutz, A., Martinerie, J., et al., Le Van Quyen, Sweeney-Reed, C.M., Nasuto, S.J., Sweeney-Reed, Witte, H., Putsche, P., Eiselt, M., Schwab, K., Wacker, M., Leistritz, L., Witte
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - This article briefs about a simulation that demonstrates how phase-locking estimates can be easily misinterpreted when accompanied by concurrent changes in signal power in the same frequency band. Considering the following simulation; 2 sinusoids oscillating at a frequency of 20 Hz, sampled at 1000 Hz over the time interval (t) -1 to 1 s, where t=0 might represent some stimulus event. For each of 100 (n) trials the sinusoids are set with a constant phase shift for the entire trial. Across trials the mean phase-shift is p/2 radians, however, to add some extra variability to the phase consistency a randomly selected phase shift is added to each trial from the Gaussian distribution. A standard time-frequency analysis is then performed on the data. Third order bi-directional butterworth filter to filter the data in 8 Hz wide bands stepping was used, in 1 Hz steps from centre frequencies of 5-55 Hz. Instantaneous spectral amplitude estimation is then performed by obtaining the absolute value of the Hilbert transformation of the data and averaging across trials. The key issue here is one of how one can interpret phase synchronisation data when concurrent changes in time-frequency power are observed. The simulated data here can be interpreted as demonstrating (significant) phase-locking (if appropriate statistical techniques are used). However, with concurrent changes in power it cannot be interpreted as an increase in phase-locking compared to the baseline, as if in response to the stimulus event. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
