A2  - Goodson, F., Goodson, Knight, M., Knight, Knight, M., Vanhoy, M., Knight, Sarter, M., Berntson, G. G., Cacioppo, J. T., Sarter, Tolman, E. C., Tolman, Uttal, W. R., Uttal, Wilson, E. O., Wilson
Y1  - 2006//
N2  - Reviews the book, A System Architecture Approach to the Brain: From Neurons to Consciousness by L. Andrew Coward (see record 2006-04140-000). In recent years, advances in cognitive neuroscience and brain imaging have morphed the mind-body problem into empirical questions concerning localized brain activity and mental functioning. In this book, Coward proposes the use of recommendation architecture as a theoretical framework from which to understand how cognition (PSI) relates to physiology (PHI). At the heart of recommendation architecture is the fact that it takes into account the need to perform many functions (i.e., process large amounts of information) with limited resources to do so and that the information generated is partially ambiguous (as opposed to operationally unambiguous, which would be a command) and thus can only be interpreted as a recommendation. Coward suggests that this element of the approach is an improvement over other approaches such as artificial neural networks, dynamical systems, and computer modeling of cognitive processes. Recommendation architecture is said to be the best way to understand how features result from the operation of the system. Additionally, he proposes that appropriate behaviors can be learned using only sensory information, rather than needing a priori knowledge of cognitive categories. The use of recommendation architecture to examine the human brain is proposed as the first step toward constructing a scientific theory that will allow the brain's processes to be described at different levels of detail in which high-level causal relationships can be mapped onto more detailed level causal relationships. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
