Y1  - 2005//
N2  - Reviews the book, Just a Little Too Thin: How to Pull Your Child Back From the Brink of an Eating Disorder by Michael Strober and Meg Schneider (see record 2005-09124-000). Among those of us who work with eating disorders, there is a mantra: "Eating disorders are not about food; they're about feelings." Michael Strober and Meg Schneider repeat this mantra throughout their book and make a convincing case for its veracity. They explain, for example, how disordered eating often arises in teenagers who are anxious and depressed and actually enables teenagers to manage these emotions. They repeatedly illustrate how success at extreme dieting offers an experience of control to teenagers when other areas of life--sexuality, friendships, school performance, family chaos--feel out of control. They elucidate so many connections between disordered eating behaviors and the regulation of difficult emotions that even the reader who merely scans this book will walk away understanding that certain eating behaviors are definitely not an expression of food-related preferences, health consciousness, or harmless dieting but are signs of psychological distress. Although this book has evident weaknesses, because of its practical bent it remains a very useful--perhaps the single most useful--how-to book for parents who are struggling with disordered eating in their teenage girls. After all, when your child is sick, your immediate and primary focus is likely to be on helping her recover. For parents who need more information about the connection between their child's eating problems and sexual abuse, spirituality, or healthy embodiment, supplemental resources are available. In short, used with supplemental information and/or professional guidance, this work is an excellent tool for parents whose daughters are just a little too thin. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
