A2  - Grassberger, P., Procaccia, I., Grassberger, Kobayashi, T., Misaki, K., Kobayashi, Pereda, E., Gamundi, A., Pereda, Rechtschaffen, A., Kales, A., Rechtschaffen
Y1  - 2002//
N1  - 26th Annual Meeting of the Japanese Society of Sleep Research. Jun, 2001. Hotel Nikko Tokyo. Tokyo. Japan. An earlier version of this article was presented at the aforementioned conference.
N2  - The interhemispheric differences of the correlation dimension (D2) in the sleep EEG of eight healthy right-handed male students (mean age 22.4 yrs) was investigated. During slow wave sleep (SWS) the D2 of the central EEG and the temporal left hemisphere (LH) EEG were significantly higher than those in the right hemisphere (RH) EEG; but during REM sleep, the D2 of the central EEG and the occipital RH EEG were significantly higher. The D2 of EEG in the left temporal site during REM sleep were significantly higher than in the right during the first and third sleep cycles, but these were significantly lower during the fourth and fifth sleep cycles. During REM sleep, temporal brain activity may shift from the LH to the RH as morning approaches. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
