A2  - Achermann, P., Hartmann, R., Gunzinger, A., Guggenbuhl, W., Borbely, A. A., Achermann, Achermann, P., Hartmann, R., Gunzinger, A., Guggenbuhl, W., Borbely, A. A., Achermann, Azumi, K., Azumi, Babloyantz, A., Destexhe, A., Babloyantz, Babloyantz, A., Destexhe, A., Babloyantz, Babloyantz, A., Nicolis, C., Salazar, U., Babloyantz, Babloyantz, A., Babloyantz, Destexhe, A., Sepulchre, J. A., Babloyantz, A., Destexhe, Dijk, D. J., Brunner, D. P., Aeschbach, D., Tobler, I., Borbely, A. A., Dijk, Eckmann, J. P., Eckmann, Freeman, W. J., Skarda, C. A., Freeman, Grassberger, P., Procaccia, I., Grassberger, Hartmann, E. L., Hartmann, Kim, Y. D., Zhuang, H. Y., Tsutsumi, M., et al., Kim, Kobayashi, T., Misaki, K., Nakagawa, H., Madokoro, S., et al., Kobayashi, Kobayashi, T., Misaki, K., Nakagawa, H., Okuda, K., et al., Kobayashi, Layne, S. P., Mayer-Kress, G., Holzfuss, J., Layne, Lorenz, E. N., Lorenz, May, R. M., May, Mendelson, W. B., Mendelson, Niestroj, E., Spieweg, I., Herrmann, W. M., Niestroj, Packard, N. H., Crutchfield, J. P., Farmer, J. D., Shaw, R. S., Packard, Parker, T. S., Shua, C. O., Parker, Pokorny, A. D., Pokorny, Pool, R., Pool, Rechtschaffen, A., Kales, A., Rechtschaffen, Renyi, A., Renyi, Roschke, J., Aldenhoff, J. B., Roschke, Roschke, J., Aldenhoff, J. B., Roschke, Roschke, J., Basar, E., Roschke, Roschke, J., Mann, K., Fell, J., Roschke, Roschke, J., Roschke, Schuster, H. G., Schuster, Shaw, R., Shaw, Takens, F., Takens
Y1  - 2002//
N2  - Investigated the influence of alcohol on sleep using the correlation dimension (D2) analysis. Polysomnography (PSG) was performed in 10 adult human males (aged 19-25 yrs) during a baseline night (BL-N) and an ethanol (0.8 g/kg body weight) night (Et-N). The mean D2 values during the Et-N and BL-N decreased significantly from wakefulness to stages 1, 2, and 3+4 of nonrapid eye movement (non-REM) sleep, and increased during REM sleep. The mean D2 of the sleep electroencephalogram (EEG) during stage 2 during the Et-N was significantly higher than during BL-N. In addition, the mean D2 values of the sleep EEG for the second, third and fourth sleep cycles during the Et-N were significantly higher than during the BL-N. These significant differences between BL-N and Et-N were not recognized by spectral and visual analyses. Our results suggest that D2 is a potentially useful parameter for quantitative analysis of the effect of ethanol on sleep EEGs throughout the entire night. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
