T1  - Scale in conscious experience: Is the brain too important to be left to specialists to study?
T2  - Scale in conscious experience: Is the brain too important to be left to specialists to study?
A1  - King, Joseph [Ed]
A1  - Pribram, Karl H [Ed]

Y1  - 1995//
N2  - The problem of scale in conscious experience is the focus of [this book]. Set against the philosophical view of "eliminative materialism," the purpose of this [volume is] to facilitate communication among investigators who approach the study of consciousness and conscious phenomena from a variety of analytical levels. The scales of analysis addressed [range] from the nanobiological scale of microtubules to the macro scale of behavioral analysis of brain lesions. The nano scale is represented by the study of processes involving the quantum physics of microtubules and their arrangement within neurons. Moving up a notch, the micro scale is represented by investigations with microelectrodes of intracortical and intrathalamic processes. The meso scale . . . is represented by the extensive analyses of the effects of brain lesions on behavior. Finally, the macro scale is represented by the influence of cognitive science on the structure and operation of conscious processes. The aim of this [book is] not simply to present an analysis of each scale, but to seek avenues whereby the different scales could be integrated with one another. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
