N1  - NATO Advanced Study Institute on Nonlinear Dynamics in the Life and Social Sciences. Apr-May, 2000. Lomonosov Moscow State University. Moscow. Russia.
N2  - The nonlinear correlates derived from deterministic chaos theory can be used to characterize sleep electroencephalograms (EEO). However, these correlates are not commonly used to determine sleep stages or to characterize other features found in these time series. This is due, in part, to a reliance on a standardized technique for scoring sleep stages. Developed in the 1960's, the technique requires the scoring by epoch. The typical EEG epoch length is 30 seconds, with a recommended digital sampling rate of at least 100 Hz, which limits the sleep stage epoch to 3000 data points. This is problematic in deterministic chaos theory due to the small sample size. In this study, 20 middle-aged subjects, free of sleep disorders and medications that could alter their sleep EEG were selected. Ten samples of artifact free 30-second sleep epochs were abstracted from each sleep stage for all 20 subjects. Nonlinear analysis of small datasets is discussed and surrogate sample sets are generated to eliminate the possibility of the more appropriate application of linear stochastic dynamics. The correlation dimension and the largest Lyapunov exponent calculated for the 30-second sleep stage epochs can be reliably measured. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
