A1  - Georgalis, Nicholas

A3  - Ellis, Ralph D [Ed], Newton, Natika [Ed]
Y1  - 2000//
N2  - Most work in cognitive science, whether computationally or biologically based, relies on the idea of 'unconscious representations.' The present article maintains that there are no such things. In contrast, conscious states do represent objects outside of the agent. The author's second main thesis concerns the 'explanatory gap' between brain states and mental states. The author proposes an analogy to enhance understanding of just what is required to close the explanatory gap, arguing that a new science is needed. Of all the currently available approaches to the study of brain activity, only nonlinear dynamics, chaos theory in particular, has a unique characteristic sufficient to close the explanatory gap. Identification of an additional feature of chaotic systems provides the framework for the explanation of another conundrum, mental causation. Furthermore, given the chaotic model of the dynamics of perception arising from W. J. Freeman's work, it appears that emotion plays a central role in perception. The application of chaos theory to brain activity dovetails with the author's first thesis, as nonlinear dynamics does not require state transitions from representations to representations, yet it has the resources to account for the representational nature of conscious states. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
