N2  - Examined EEG criteria concerning the neurological prognosis of normality in very premature infants. Neonatal EEGs of 17 neurologically normal, very premature infants (conceptional aged 26-28 wks) were recorded during the 1st wk and the 15th and 21st days of life. Normality of Ss was defined as normal neonatal cranial ultrasound scans and normal neurological outcome at a minimum postnatal age of 2 yrs. Results show that all EEG tracings were discontinuous. Bursts were interhemispherically synchronous and lasted up to 3 min. EEG patterns consisted mainly of slow waves, with high amplitude, occipital predominance, and superimposed alpha, beta, and theta rhythms. High amplitude theta rhythms predominated in temporal areas. Beta rhythms were primarily localized in central areas and alpha rhythms were primarily localized in central and occipital areas. Sleep state differentiation began as early as 26 wks of conceptional age. It is concluded that EEG data show constant and reproducible patterns in normal very premature infants that can constitute a basis for the determination of EEG criteria for neurological prognosis in infants conceptional aged 26-28 wks. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
