T1  - Alterations of continuous MEG measures during mental activities.
A1  - Fell, Jurgen
A1  - Roschke, Joachim
A1  - Grozinger, Michael
A1  - Hinrichs, Hermann
A1  - Heinze, Hans-Jochen

Y1  - 2000//
N2  - Investigated the topography of 11 continuous magnetencephalography (MEG) measures for the eyes-opened and eyes-closed condition together with three simple mental tasks (mental arithmetic, visual imagery, word generation). One minute recordings for each condition from 16 Ss (mean age 29.3 yrs) were analyzed. The electrophysiological measures consisted of 6 spectral band measures together with spectral edge frequency and spectral entropy, plus the time-domain-based entropy of amplitudes (ENA) and the nonlinear measures correlation dimension D2 and Lyapunov exponent L1. Results indicated a pronounced task-dependent difference between the anterior and posterior region, but no lateralization effects. Although the nonlinear measures ranged in the middle field with respect to the number of significant contrasts, they were the only ones to be partially successful in discriminating the mental tasks from each other. It is concluded that the most efficient measure turned out to be the ENA, as under mental activation the ENA was larger in both no task conditions (eyes opened and eyes closed). This reflects lower variations of the maximum amplitude during performance of mental tasks than during no task states. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
