T1  - Discrimination of sleep stages: A comparison between spectral and nonlinear EEG measures.
T3  - Clinical Neurophysiology
A1  - Fell, Jurgen
A1  - Roschke, Joachim
A1  - Mann, Klaus
A1  - Schaffner, Cornelius

Y1  - 1996//
N2  - Compared the ability of several spectral and nonlinear EEG measures to discriminate different sleep stages, evaluating the stochastic time domain based measure entropy of amplitudes. These measures were calculated for 12 male 23-36 yr olds from sleep EEG segments of 2:44 min duration, each segment unambiguously corresponding to one of the sleep stages I, II, SWS and REM. Generally, nonlinear measures (correlation dimension D2 and largest Lyapunov exponent Ll) performed better in discriminating sleep stages I and II, whereas spectral measures showed advantages in discriminating stage II and SWS. Combinations of spectral and nonlinear measures yielded a better overall discrimination of sleep stages than did spectral measures alone. The best overall discrimination was reached even without inclusion of any of the spectral measures. Results confirm the importance and practical reliability of the application of nonlinear methods to EEG analysis. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
