T1  - Nonlinear EEG dynamics during sleep in depression and schizophrenia.
A1  - Roschke, Joachim
A1  - Mann, K
A1  - Fell, J

Y1  - 1994//
N2  - Analyzed EEG segments of 16,384 data points (2.44 min) corresponding to sleep stages and REM to calculate the correlation dimension D2 and the principal Lyapunov-exponent lambda1. Ss were 9 unmedicated depressive inpatients, 11 unmedicated schizophrenic inpatients, and matched controls. It is proposed that D2 and lambda1 are correlated with complexity and flexibility of information processing. Results point to an alteration of the nonlinear brain dynamics mainly during the slow-wave stages in depressed Ss, whereas in schizophrenics alterations were mainly observed during REM sleep. A disturbed slow-wave sleep may be an expression of the seriously impaired physical constitution in depression. Reduced correlation dimensions and increased principal Lyapunov exponents during REM sleep may correlate with impaired information processing found in schizophrenia. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
