T1  - A nonlinear approach to brain function: Deterministic chaos and sleep EEG.
T3  - Sleep: Journal of Sleep and Sleep Disorders Research
A1  - Roschke, J
A1  - Aldenhoff, J. B

Y1  - 1992//
N2  - Demonstrated that the EEG during different sleep stages is a deterministic process. Sleep EEG was recorded from 12 male adults (aged 20-31 yrs) under drug-free conditions (placebo) and after 2.5 mg lorazepam po. In normal healthy probands, the influence of lorazepam did not alter the dimensionality of slow-wave sleep, but it did alter that of Stage II and REM sleep. The different sleep stages corresponded to significantly different correlation dimensions. The more the sleep moved toward slow-wave sleep, the lower the correlation dimension D2. Further studies are needed to indicate how dimensionality and dynamics of EEG may be changed under sleep disturbances such as in depressive or psychotic disorder and how pharmaceuticals with different psychotropic effects influence the dynamical properties of the central nervous system (CNS). (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
